---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getGitHubEditUrl, getGitHubRepositoryUrl } from "../lib/github";

const editUrl = getGitHubEditUrl("src/pages/getting-started.astro");
const repositoryUrl = getGitHubRepositoryUrl();
---

<Layout title="Getting started" description="Fork Astro Pages Publisher, configure your content, and publish it with GitHub Pages.">
  <article class="docs-page editable-block">
    <p class="eyebrow">Your own site in a few steps</p>
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      Astro Pages Publisher is designed to be forked. The GitHub owner, repository name,
      deployment URL, base path, profile links, and edit links are detected at build time.
      You do not need to replace a hardcoded account name throughout the source.
    </p>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>1. Fork and rename the repository</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the <a href={repositoryUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">repository running this demo</a> and choose <strong>Fork</strong>.</li>
        <li>Choose your GitHub account or organization as the owner.</li>
        <li>Rename the fork to the repository name you want to publish, for example <code>my-site</code>.</li>
        <li>Keep the default branch as <code>main</code>, or update your workflow settings if you deliberately use another branch.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The site URL is generated from the repository context. A project repository is published under <code>https://OWNER.github.io/REPOSITORY/</code>; a repository named <code>OWNER.github.io</code> uses the domain root.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>2. Enable GitHub Pages</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open your fork's <strong>Settings → Pages</strong>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Build and deployment</strong>, select <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> as the source.</li>
        <li>In <strong>Settings → Actions → General</strong>, allow the workflow permissions needed by the deployment and template-restoration jobs. The workflow declares the minimum permissions per job.</li>
        <li>Push a commit to your default branch, or run <strong>Deploy to GitHub Pages</strong> manually from the Actions tab.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The workflow validates templates and content, runs Astro checks and tests, builds the static site, scans the output, and deploys only the verified artifact. Read <a href="../how-it-works/">How it works</a> for the full pipeline and architecture.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>3. Personalize the site</h2>
      <p>Start with <code>src/content/site.json</code>. It controls the brand, description, short introduction, and footer text. It is intentionally separate from the GitHub account identity.</p>
      <p>Review the included Markdown posts in <code>src/content/blog/</code>: keep them as starter examples, adapt them, or replace them with your own articles. Edit the pages in <code>src/pages/</code> and add footer links under <code>src/pages/footer/</code>. Navigation is generated from the pages that exist.</p>
      <p>See <a href="../templates/">Templates</a> for the supported starter files and their rename rules.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>4. Run it locally</h2>
      <p>Install Node.js 22.23.4 or newer, then clone your fork and install its locked dependencies:</p>
      <pre><code>npm ci
npm run dev</code></pre>
      <p>No <code>.env</code> file is needed for a standard checkout. The project reads the repository from the <code>origin</code> Git remote and the active branch from Git automatically. GitHub Actions uses the repository and branch supplied by its workflow context. An optional <code>.env</code> file is only needed for custom overrides such as a custom domain or repository filters.</p>
      <p>Before pushing changes, run the full verification pipeline:</p>
      <pre><code>npm run verify</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section docs-callout">
      <h2>Before using this as a public website</h2>
      <p>The Legal Notice page is included as an editable scaffold, not as ready-to-publish legal copy. Replace every <code>[REQUIRED ...]</code> and <code>[IF APPLICABLE: ...]</code> prompt, remove optional sections that do not apply, and check the rules for your jurisdiction and activity. After review, remove the starter warning and change its status marker from <code>incomplete</code> to <code>complete</code>. The content and build checks reject a completed status while template prompts remain. This workflow does not replace qualified legal advice.</p>
    </section>

    <a href={editUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="edit-pencil" title="Edit this page">✎</a>
  </article>
</Layout>
